    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "24c9e69c_39706761",
        "filename": "graphics/java/android/graphics/Canvas.java",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 100,
      "author": {
        "id": 1042828
      },
      "writtenOn": "2015-09-09T02:33:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "OK.  Thanks.  That unfortunately makes sense.  But it seems quite unpleasant.  Any Java code that accesses a Canvas from its finalizer is likely to result in native heap corruption.  That doesn\u0027t seem like a very unlikely application bug to me.\n\nI don\u0027t see how to fix this without ether adding a level of indirection to all the mNativeCanvasWrapper accesses or adding some runtime hook.\n\nI think Richard Uhler is planning on adding some support for running really restricted native-only finalizers directly from the GC, and not delaying reclamation?  This seems to argue that that mechanism should be general enough to clear a Java field and invoke a native finalizer.  That way we can lose CanvasFinalizer here, and clear the real mNativeCanvasWrapper to make this fail cleanly.  Does that make sense?",
      "parentUuid": "93a1f560_6b99cd55",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 100,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 100,
        "endChar": 31
      },
      "revId": "66367338ef0b7cfd6f445a73c216c9f1911826d0",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "84d4522f_df3cb7ad",
        "filename": "graphics/java/android/graphics/Canvas.java",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 100,
      "author": {
        "id": 1010103
      },
      "writtenOn": "2015-09-09T14:43:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "My dream would be something like this:\n\n@Destructor(nDestroy)\nprivate final long mNativePtr;\n\nprivate static native void nDestroy(long nativePtr);\n\nWith the runtime invoking the destructor and passing in the value of the decorated field at the time the object itself is freed. Or farmed off to a different thread, but after the class instance is freed is more what I\u0027m after so I never have to worry about being accessed in weird states.",
      "parentUuid": "24c9e69c_39706761",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 100,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 100,
        "endChar": 31
      },
      "revId": "66367338ef0b7cfd6f445a73c216c9f1911826d0",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "44cedaa4_481c6f72",
        "filename": "graphics/java/android/graphics/Canvas.java",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 100,
      "author": {
        "id": 1042828
      },
      "writtenOn": "2015-09-09T22:24:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This raises an interesting question: When should nDestroy be called?  I assumed that it would be called at the same time as the object would become finalizable, but that actually seems wrong.  It should probably happen at PhantomReference processing time, i.e. when the object is really dead.  That should normally be at the same time. But it means that if someone touches an otherwise unreachable Canvas in a finalizer, things just work.  And we no longer need to worry about clearing these fields.",
      "parentUuid": "84d4522f_df3cb7ad",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 100,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 100,
        "endChar": 31
      },
      "revId": "66367338ef0b7cfd6f445a73c216c9f1911826d0",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fa582b71_80d73a50",
        "filename": "graphics/java/android/graphics/Interpolator.java",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 155,
      "author": {
        "id": 1010118
      },
      "writtenOn": "2014-11-12T22:51:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "by making this non-final then it\u0027d be possible for shared instances of Interpolator to not have a native_instance field that\u0027s assigned - as the constructor fence goes missing. Is this any less of a potential bug than the potential finalizer bug?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 155,
        "startChar": 11,
        "endLine": 155,
        "endChar": 12
      },
      "revId": "66367338ef0b7cfd6f445a73c216c9f1911826d0",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3afba387_ef0c1ddd",
        "filename": "graphics/java/android/graphics/Interpolator.java",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 155,
      "author": {
        "id": 1042828
      },
      "writtenOn": "2014-11-13T00:19:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Note that I\u0027m touching a lot of classes here whose semantics I don\u0027t fully understand.  Hence the code review is appreciated.\n\nHaving said that, I think the implicit contract for library classes is that if the client lets the constructor race with a use, all bets are off.  A few exceptional classes like String promise more than that, primarily because the so-called Java security model breaks completely if they don\u0027t.  I don\u0027t see why this class should promise more than the default.\n\nTechnically it doesn\u0027t anyway, since it has non-final fields which are not guaranteed to be stable in such cases.  In practice, I agree that we\u0027re weakening a property of the class, but I don\u0027t think it\u0027s a useful or expected one.  If you hand off an instance of this class to another thread, you should do it using mutual exclusion or a volatile reference, in which case none of this matters much.\n\nI agree that in the presence of a racing access you may briefly see native_instance point to junk, and could still get memory corruption, if you time things just right.  But in such a case, it should be much more likely that you see null, and hence get an easily debuggable crash.  Currently, if we have another finalizable object pointing to one of these, and the other finalizer tries to update this object, we have a 50-50 chance of it working correctly or of memory corruption, with no chance of an easily debuggable crash.",
      "parentUuid": "fa582b71_80d73a50",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 155,
        "startChar": 11,
        "endLine": 155,
        "endChar": 12
      },
      "revId": "66367338ef0b7cfd6f445a73c216c9f1911826d0",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
